{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Babel for supporting PBS. \nDon't panic, but it's possible that an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon is forming right behind you right \nnow. Don't bother looking. You won't see it. It"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11760,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "definitely won't look like the classic picture \nof the black hole that you're probably familiar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15200,
    "dDurationMs": 5583,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with. You won't even know that it's growing \nto envelop you until it's way too late."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, a couple of quick \nannouncements. First, we have some new data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liking and commenting really does help get the \nepisodes shared. So, you know, please do both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those things. And we've also learned that \nthe number one reason that people support us on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patreon isn't actually the perks. It's simply to \nsupport the Spacetime community and the work that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50160,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we do. So, for those of you who do support, thank \nyou so so much. But don't get me wrong, there are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perks and they're good. There's a link in the \ndescription if you'd like to join. This really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be a huge help. Next up, we're excited \nto announce our new Rocket Equation coffee mug"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so you can know exactly how much caffeine you need \nto achieve escape velocity. We also still have our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fates of the Universe UV Glow T-shirt and hoodie, \nwhich lights up after sun exposure. So whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80080,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you're rooting for the big rip, the big crunch, \nor the big freeze, you can wear your prediction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the universe's ultimate fate. Now onto the \nepisode. By now, we all have an idea of what black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes are supposed to look like. Discs of absolute \ndarkness on the sky, surrounded by gravity warped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "starfields, and the final electromagnetic screams \nof stuff about to fall in. We think of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole as this roughly spherical surface of \nlightlessness. And the name we give that surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the event horizon. If I were to ask where \nis the black hole, you might point to the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. And that would be fair because a black \nhole is really defined as something with an event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. The surface of no escape surrounding \na region so packed with mass and energy that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even light becomes trapped. The event horizon is \nas good a wear as anything for the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if I were to ask what is the black hole, it \nseems fair to answer anything inside that horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it turns out these answers are somewhere \nbetween inadequate and just plain wrong. Even the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "question, when does the horizon exist isn't that \nsimple. Event horizons are actually much harder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to pin down both in space and time. And when it \ncomes to black holes, a little bit of ambiguity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156160,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can get you into a lot of trouble. So for safety \nsake, let's get clear about the event horizon. If"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we define the event horizon as that surface from \nwhich light cannot escape, we need to ask escape"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to where? One pretty reasonable answer would be \nto require that in order to be considered above"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon, light has to be able to truly \nescape, which means it must be able to get far"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough away from the black hole that it no longer \nis affected by its gravity. This is actually close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the formal definition. Light must be able to \nreach infinite distance which is physics ees for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very very far away. But this definition is already \nat odds with how we tend to imagine the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon as the scary black disc or sphere. Let's \napproach that disc then to investigate. We'll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel to the very near vicinity of what we think \nof as an event horizon. Now, after collecting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some Hawking radiation or whatever, we switch on \nour ridiculously powerful rockets to move away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because we carefully stayed above what looks to us \nlike the horizon, we're able to make some headway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then, misfortune strikes. A giant asteroid \nor something falls into the black hole from above"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us and the black hole grows in mass and the event \nhorizon expands and we're enveloped. But according"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to our earlier definition, if we or if light \ncannot escape to an extreme distance, then we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were already beneath the event horizon and we're \nalready doomed. Yes, something that black surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "did expand to envelop us, but it wasn't the true \nevent horizon at all. So, let's dig a little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deeper into the formal definition of the event \nhorizon because it completely changes the what,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where, and even the when of our understanding of a \nblack hole. The textbook definition, the one that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steven Hawking and George Ellis sit down in their \nbook, The Large Scale Structure of Spacetime,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is as follows. An event horizon is the boundary \nof the causal past of future null infinity. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causal past of future null infinity. Okay, super \nhelpful. Well, we can break it down maybe a little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further. We'll use a Penrose diagram. Longtime \nviewers will remember these and it'll make"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things a little clearer, hopefully. Before we \ncomplicate things by adding black holes, here's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Penrose diagram of an infinite flat universe. \nTime increases upwards and one dimension of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is traced horizontally, but the contours of space \nand time are curved so that the path of light is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always 45°. And those lines are also increasingly \nsquished together towards the edges so that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite space fits in this finite map. The points \nat the top and bottom represent our infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future and past. And the left and right points are \ninfinite spatial boundaries. But it's the diagonal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "edges of the diagram that we need to pay attention \nto. These are the null infinities that Hawking and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ellis are talking about. In particular, the \nfuture null infinity is where light rays end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the infinite future. And the past null infinity \nis where they appear to come from if we trace them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back in time forever. Now we can add a black \nhole to the Penrose diagram like this. This"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is an eternal black hole. It was always there and \nalways will be there. It's just sitting out there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to our left and any motion in that direction will \neventually reach it. And that means we can replace"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one future null infinity by the event horizon. \nBelow the event horizon, space and time grid lines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually switch place. Moving up on this diagram \nis equivalent to falling deeper in the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the inevitable destination of the black \nhole center. the singularity which occupies all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future times inside the black hole. We have \nprevious episodes that explain all of this in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more detail. With this sort of diagram, we can see \nthat nothing can escape the black hole. In order"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to escape, something would need to take a path \nshallower than 45° and that means going faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396640,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light. Even light gets stuck forever at the \nevent horizon if it starts there and inevitably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the singularity even if it was trying to \nmove outwards. Hawking and Ellis's definition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is clearer in the context of this diagram. They \ndefine an event horizon as the boundary of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causal past of future null infinity. This is \nthe future null infinity and its causal past is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anything that can get to it traveling no greater \nthan light speed. That means the entire universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "except the region below the event horizon. Nothing \nbehind that boundary can affect future infinity or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indeed anything else in the universe that isn't \nitself behind the same event horizon. Okay, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it feels like we're saying the same thing that we \nalready knew but with more words and more lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But crucially, we we've now been precise enough \nabout black holes as a causal structure. There's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something that determines what events are allowed \nand forbidden in the universe. In doing this,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we remove a lot of ambiguity that we ran into \nearlier. But we also discover something really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strange. The event horizon definition is no longer \nlocal. It doesn't refer to a single location in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space or point in time. It's the boundary below \nwhich neither you nor light rays can escape to a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great distance, aka reach future null infinity, \neven if the black hole that eventually traps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you hasn't started forming yet. So the event \nhorizon is a property of the entire spaceime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaching all the way into infinite future. It's \nnot just a statement about what is possible now,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but what will be possible based on everything that \nwill happen to this patch of spaceime. Physicists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sometimes describe the event horizon as being \nteological from the ancient Greek term telos"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning final purpose. It tells us about the final \nstate of the universe, the paths that make it out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to infinity and those that eventually end up stuck \ninside the black hole. That's what I mean when I"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say that an event horizon might have just passed \nyou right now and you wouldn't even realize. Let's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do another thought experiment to see why. We'll \nstart back on Earth. Relatively flat space free of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes. However, for unfortunate reasons that \nwe don't need to get into, but may involve vogons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's a spherical shell of radiation of light \ncollapsing towards us. This is an example of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537680,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Va spacetime devised by Indian physicist Prahalad \nVaja as a toy model to explore spaceimes and event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizons of a collapsing star without all of \nthe messy matter. In this thought experiment,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it feels like there's no event horizon as the \nshell of radiation converges on us. We don't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even know that it's coming because no signal can \ntravel faster than the radiation zone collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's ultimately going to form a black hole with \na radius governed by the amount of energy in that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation. But according to our formal definition, \nthe event horizon forms long before the black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole. In fact, it grows from the center of the \ncollapsing sphere at the speed of light, reaching"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its maximum size when it intercepts the infalling \nradiation. That's when the black hole truly forms,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the horizon was already there. To visualize \nthis, let's say that you're at the center of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapsing shell and there's a lastditch effort \nto escape. You shoot a lighteed signal out into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space to see if any passing spaceships will \nbeam you up. If your signal makes it beyond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ultimate size of the event horizon before \nthe incoming radiation crosses that same point,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it escapes and maybe you hit your way out of \nthere. But if not, then it and you remain stuck"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind the horizon. If you send your signal too \nlate, then it never reaches future null infinity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So by our definition, it was always behind the \ntrue event horizon, trailing it as it expands"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to become a black hole. Let's see what the Penrose \ndiagram looks like for a Vega spacetime. It starts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like our black hole-free diagram, which we'll \ncut in half because who cares about this entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side of the universe? The sphere of collapsing \nradiation looks like this single line in our one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimension of space. It eventually reaches us, but \nthe black hole it forms comes fully into existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when it collapses below that certain radius. The \nevent horizon is then a diagonal line upwards from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that point. Just as in our black hole containing \nPenrose diagram, the spaceime within that event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667040,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon is terminated by a singularity. Now, it's \neasy to see which light rays escape the forming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon and which don't. Ones that we send \nout early enough to reach future null infinity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the ones that escape. Those that depart too \nlate fail to pass the involing radiation before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole forms. But these rays were doomed \nfrom the very start. And so we need to trace the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true event horizon backwards to encompass this \nentire region. We have a part of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is trapped even though it doesn't know that \nit's in a black hole. So yeah, a nasonent event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon might be forming behind your couch as \nwe speak. You might want to check that out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although you'll have to wait until the end of the \nuniverse before you can be absolutely certain. A"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "secret event horizon could be due to the collapse \nof something huge, a giant region of the galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or even the universe billions of years from now. \nAll of this is a weird way to think about event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729040,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizons, but does anything really change? Who \ncares if we can't find the horizon? Well, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most obvious problem is that an event horizon is \nused to define the boundary of a black hole. So,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741120,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it makes it hard to talk about where a black hole \nis. It contradicts our astrophysical intuition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that there is a black hole at the center \nof our galaxy called Sagittarius A star and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars will get eaten if they get within a certain \ndistance. But this local description feels at odds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the teological definition. This is even more \napparent when we try to simulate the merges of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes on a computer. Because Einstein's \nequations are difficult to work with in these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highly dynamical regimes, we rely on simulations \nto tell us what the spaceime will look like, we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "set up Einstein's equations to describe two black \nholes in spiraling, then evolve the simulation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward in time until the black holes merge, and \nwe're left with a single black hole. But where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790560,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and with what shape are the event horizons of the \nmerging black holes and of the final result? If"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a horizon is teological, then it's defined across \nthe entirety of the simulation to future infinity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could get a single time slice out to see how \nspacetime has evolved, but it won't tell us where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810000,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the horizon is. We could possibly trace light rays \nback in time from the end of the simulation and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look for the boundary of a region from which no \nlight gets out alive. And that's very much in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spirit of Hawking and Ellis's definition. But this \nis very awkward as a way to find black holes and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not necessarily very instructive if we care about \nthe local shape of spaceime during the simulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And besides, we still want to know what to call \nthat black surface that we think we're escaping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but are really doomed because the real event \nhorizon is already above us. In the special"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case of a black hole that's done forming and will \nnever absorb new stuff, that black surface really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the event horizon in the technical sense that \nwe just defined. But in the real universe where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes tend to keep growing, we need another \nname for the current snapshot of the black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862560,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boundary. And that name is the apparent horizon \nwhere the formal event horizon represents the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "global inescapable boundary integrated across all \nfuture time. The apparent horizon is a more local"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876000,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beast. It's the locally inescapable boundary, \nthe thing that you can't exit even right now,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regardless of whether you can make an ultimate \nescape. The formal definition of the apparent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon is also in terms of what light rays can \ndo. But now it's not whether they can reach future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "null infinity. It's whether they can make any \nprogress whatsoever in the outward direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Technically, we say that the apparent horizon \nis the outermost surface from which all outward-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905600,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "facing null geodysics are converging inwards. \nHaving a local definition of the horizon is nice,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially because it lets us understand what \nthings look like if we're not at null infinity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or otherwise really far away. When we see these \nawesome simulations of merging black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we're seeing is the evolution of the apparent \nhorizon. So why bother with the first definition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the event horizon at all? Well, it's because \nthe apparent horizon is relative. Every observer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will agree on the definition of the true event \nhorizon, the ultimately inescapable region. But"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the definition of the apparent horizon varies not \njust over time, but also with frame of reference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950000,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In relativity, your sense of now varies with your \nvelocity and the curvature of spaceime. Each sense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the present is a 3D spatial slice out of \nthe four-dimensional spaceime. And that slicing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes with perspective. The apparent horizon \nwill also change between these slicings. And it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even possible to find slicings or definitions of a \nspecially extended now that include a black hole,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in which the apparent horizon disappears. \nBut that's not true of the true event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you're behind that, it doesn't matter how \nyou slice spacetime, you are stuck. In fact,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987920,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every possible apparent horizon is guaranteed to \nalways lie inside or on the actual event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this sense, the apparent horizon gives a \nconservative estimate about the extent of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole. It also gives a much more useful way \nto study the behavior of dynamical black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006480,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes that are currently feeding like in \nquazars or in merging black holes. In that case,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the apparent horizon does something quite \nunexpected. You have these apparent horizons for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inspiraling black holes that warp as they get \nclose, but the apparent horizon of the final isn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a smooth merging of the two. That larger \njoint apparent horizon forms only in the instant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the event horizons merge. So, the apparent \nhorizon is far from a substitute for the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. It's a genuinely different object with \nits own interesting properties. So, what, where,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and when is an event horizon? And is there one \nforming behind you right now? Well, if you care"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about what's happening close to a black hole at \na particular time, then you need to get creative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You need to choose reference frames and ask where \nfor that observer light appears to turn inwards at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the apparent horizon. But if you want the most \nclear definition, the definition that matters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you want to escape a black hole, then the true \nevent horizon is not a property of local space or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immediate time. It's a thing defined by the past \nand the future and exists as a causal boundary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cutting its way through spaceime. Thank you to \nBubble for supporting PBS. If you spend any time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "online, you might have found that today's world \nis highly international. That's why Babel teaches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you how to have real world conversations through \nbite-sized lessons designed by over 650 language"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experts. To make learning more accessible with \nBabel's group plan, each subscription comes with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to six unique accounts with access to all \navailable languages. You can share up to five"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accounts and everyone you share with can learn \nany Babel language at their own pace. Whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's just you or up to five family members, \nfriends, or co-workers, the Babel group plan"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "includes access to byite-size lessons, bingeable \npodcasts, and conversation practice. Here it is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right here. Restaurant means, do you want to \ngo to the restaurant? So, if you're interested"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in learning a new language and sharing that with \nfive other people in your life, check out the link"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136880,
    "dDurationMs": 21120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the description for the Babel Group plan. \nYou can get up to 55% off your subscription."
    } ]
  } ]
}
